From: FlipperEmu
Subject: [PATCH] chardev: do not log what a busy backend did not take

qemu_chr_write_buffer() logs what the backend wrote. When the backend
wrote nothing and returned an error, it logs the whole buffer, on the
ground that a fatal error means the caller will not come again with it.
EAGAIN is not such an error: a front end that gets it sets up a watch and
writes the same bytes again when the backend can take them, and they are
logged then. So every EAGAIN put its bytes into the log file twice.

Log the buffer on an error only when the error is not EAGAIN.

Seen with the Flipper Zero model: its serial port on LPUART1 is a hub of a
pty and a socket, with a log file. When the program at the socket did not
read for a moment and the socket's buffer filled (after 277 characters,
since the UART model writes them one at a time), the UART waited, as it
should, the program got each character once, and the log file had the one
that waited twice.

Signed-off-by: FlipperEmu
---
--- a/chardev/char.c
+++ b/chardev/char.c
@@ -179,11 +179,13 @@
          * method, thus we'll log the remainder at that time.
          */
         qemu_chr_write_log(s, buf, *offset);
-    } else if (res < 0) {
+    } else if (res < 0 && errno != EAGAIN) {
         /*
          * If a fatal error was reported by the backend,
          * assume this method won't be invoked again with
-         * this buffer, so log it all right away.
+         * this buffer, so log it all right away. (EAGAIN is
+         * none: the caller comes again with the same bytes,
+         * and they are logged when they are written.)
          */
         qemu_chr_write_log(s, buf, len);
     }
